      <data key="d2">a33174286d9d6731d658d04ab0089fcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WWW.GUTENBERG.ORG/DONATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The donation webpage where supporters can contribute funds to support Project Gutenberg's mission."</data>
      <data key="d2">a33174286d9d6731d658d04ab0089fcc</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="&quot;BOB CRATCHIT&quot;" target="&quot;TINY TIM&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Bob Cratchit is the father of Tiny Tim, and he cares deeply for Tiny Tim's well-being. His concern for Tiny Tim's health and happiness plays a significant role in the story's moral, highlighting themes of parental love and compassion.</data>
      <data key="d5">696cd4a0ae22e395f3c9d04cfcea8d49,f13768b850451c6d88dc4c794e5c6cf7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;BOB CRATCHIT&quot;" target="&quot;CRATCHIT FAMILY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Bob is the head of the Cratchit family, central to their collective identity and struggles."|</data>
      <data key="d5">696cd4a0ae22e395f3c9d04cfcea8d49</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;BOB CRATCHIT&quot;" target="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scrooge is Bob Cratchit's employer, and their relationship is central to the story, highlighting themes of kindness and social responsibility. Initially, Scrooge is portrayed as a miserly and unkind employer, but after experiencing a change of heart, he decides to improve his treatment of Bob Cratchit and support his family. This transformation underscores the story's message about the importance of compassion and social conscience.</data>
      <data key="d5">4d24514bfd653ec63181d9d824eb98ef,f13768b850451c6d88dc4c794e5c6cf7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST&quot;" target="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Ghost of Christmas Past is a supernatural entity that appears to Ebenezer Scrooge as a guide to his own past. It has a fluctuating form and emits a bright jet of light from its head, indicating its supernatural nature. The Ghost of Christmas Past leads Scrooge through memories of his earlier life, including joyful celebrations such as Fezziwig's party, encouraging him to reflect on his life and the choices he has made. Its purpose is to prompt self-examination and potential change by revealing scenes from Scrooge's past.</data>
      <data key="d5">312a70793b39dccac6fb4e4b0d23df0d,4b5a2c29bc3f579c8040c45f2a9363d0,6359ab00dbbec9c39edad51589aea3ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST&quot;" target="&quot;ROOM&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Ghost manifests in Scrooge's room, where the supernatural visitation begins."|</data>
      <data key="d5">6359ab00dbbec9c39edad51589aea3ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT&quot;" target="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Ghost guides Scrooge through scenes of Christmas cheer, encouraging his transformation from miser to benefactor."|</data>
      <data key="d5">adad8724255cb219e0e4760c3773404c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME&quot;" target="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Ghost shows Scrooge his future, including his own death and the fate of others, prompting his change of heart."</data>
      <data key="d5">4dd973f86fac64800eb57dc9848d3136</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MRS. CRATCHIT&quot;" target="&quot;BOB&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Bob and Mrs. Cratchit are family members working together to prepare and enjoy the Christmas dinner."</data>
      <data key="d5">6b5812a07d880d92f055d038934b2b87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MRS. CRATCHIT&quot;" target="&quot;PUDDING&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mrs. Cratchit prepares the pudding, which is celebrated as a successful dish."</data>
      <data key="d5">6b5812a07d880d92f055d038934b2b87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;MARLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marley was Scrooge's business partner, and his death profoundly affects Scrooge's outlook and the progression of the story. Marley's face appears as a ghostly image on the knocker, symbolizing his lingering presence and influence. Additionally, Marley's ghost appears to Scrooge, serving as a supernatural warning about his life choices and future.</data>
      <data key="d5">2133f23cd48da7db45725d6fd180a9a6,5e8b09e4b9e2e758c5a254dc558da1dc,e7b8bfb3e2c427ea3b889edc137f6ec5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;OLD MARLEY'S FUNERAL&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge attends and is directly affected by Marley's funeral, which marks a pivotal moment in the narrative."|</data>
      <data key="d5">5e8b09e4b9e2e758c5a254dc558da1dc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;SCROOGE AND MARLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The firm of Scrooge and Marley signifies their business partnership, which continues to influence the story's events and themes."|</data>
      <data key="d5">5e8b09e4b9e2e758c5a254dc558da1dc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;NEPHEW&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The nephew is a family member who shares in Christmas festivities and games with Scrooge, embodying familial warmth and joy. He actively attempts to persuade Scrooge to embrace the spirit of Christmas by being more generous and forgiving. The nephew visits Scrooge to invite him to celebrate Christmas, representing kindness and familial warmth, and serving as a contrast to Scrooge's initial attitude towards the holiday.</data>
      <data key="d5">7873ef9345f51b5cea209977c77a2895,8b240ac8efc37250a30c472cdc63f1ff,adad8724255cb219e0e4760c3773404c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;CLERK&quot;">
      <data key="d3">4.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scrooge employs the clerk in his counting-house, and their relationship is characterized by Scrooge's stinginess and the clerk's modest resignation, patience, and modesty. The clerk's well-being and attitude are influenced by Scrooge's treatment and attitude towards money and service. Additionally, the clerk's demeanor and circumstances reflect the typical experience of a common worker in Victorian society, and he is affected by Scrooge's attitude as well as the Christmas spirit.</data>
      <data key="d5">0199efcc183aac09eefc978fa1b715f1,312a70793b39dccac6fb4e4b0d23df0d,7873ef9345f51b5cea209977c77a2895,8b240ac8efc37250a30c472cdc63f1ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;CITY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scrooge's counting-house is located within the City, which serves as the urban backdrop and atmospheric setting for the story. The City provides the primary setting for much of the story's action, illustrating a lively Christmas atmosphere that enhances the narrative's festive and bustling environment.</data>
      <data key="d5">33439623114354941f9323e7d62c790b,8b240ac8efc37250a30c472cdc63f1ff</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;PORTLY GENTLEMEN&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The portly gentlemen visit Scrooge to solicit donations for the poor, highlighting social charity during Christmas."</data>
      <data key="d5">7873ef9345f51b5cea209977c77a2895</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;MR. MARLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mr. Marley's ghost appears to Scrooge to warn him about his miserly ways and to urge him to change."</data>
      <data key="d5">7873ef9345f51b5cea209977c77a2895</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;GENTLEMAN&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The gentleman seeks to persuade Scrooge to contribute to charity, highlighting social responsibility and compassion."|</data>
      <data key="d5">41f7fa53e3b8823756ff8863b6d8ca0c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;MARLEY'S FACE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Marley's face on the knocker signifies the supernatural warning from the ghost of Marley, indicating a connection between Scrooge and the ghostly apparition."|</data>
      <data key="d5">0199efcc183aac09eefc978fa1b715f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;LONDON&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge's residence and workplace are located in London, making the city the setting for his life and the story's events."|</data>
      <data key="d5">0199efcc183aac09eefc978fa1b715f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;CORPORATION&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The corporation is part of the societal context surrounding Scrooge, representing societal institutions involved in commerce and governance."</data>
      <data key="d5">2133f23cd48da7db45725d6fd180a9a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;ALDERMEN&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Aldermen are societal officials mentioned alongside other institutions, indicating the societal environment of the story."</data>
      <data key="d5">2133f23cd48da7db45725d6fd180a9a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;LIVERY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Livery is part of the societal institutions mentioned, representing trade or craft guilds."</data>
      <data key="d5">2133f23cd48da7db45725d6fd180a9a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;HOUSE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The house is the setting where Scrooge's supernatural encounter occurs, including the hall, rooms, and staircase."</data>
      <data key="d5">2133f23cd48da7db45725d6fd180a9a6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;FIRE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The fire in Scrooge's room is a setting where the ghost appears and the supernatural events unfold."|</data>
      <data key="d5">e7b8bfb3e2c427ea3b889edc137f6ec5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;BELL&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The swinging and ringing of the bell signifies the supernatural presence and the ghost's arrival."|</data>
      <data key="d5">e7b8bfb3e2c427ea3b889edc137f6ec5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;DOOR&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The door is the physical entry point for the ghost, marking the boundary between the natural and supernatural realms."|</data>
      <data key="d5">e7b8bfb3e2c427ea3b889edc137f6ec5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;JACOB MARLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Jacob Marley is the deceased former business partner of Ebenezer Scrooge. As a ghostly spirit, Marley appears to Scrooge to warn him about the negative consequences of his greed and selfishness. His ghostly visitation serves both as a warning and an instruction, aiming to prompt Scrooge to reconsider his life choices and embrace a more compassionate and generous outlook. Marley's appearance as a spiritual visitor acts as a catalyst for Scrooge's eventual transformation.</data>
      <data key="d5">7c3c14fb7534245699334c3f3af741d1,a9d95369d1d8bbd8108e7bbd93d652ec,e63efed56e4606f35b676ab40e2432aa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;GHOST&quot;">
      <data key="d3">5.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Ghost is a supernatural entity that interacts with Scrooge, serving as a moral guide and catalyst for his transformation. It visits Scrooge by entering through his door and influences his perceptions of the supernatural realm. The Ghost guides and shows visions to Scrooge, revealing insights into his past, present, and future, with the purpose of inspiring moral change. As a spirit that teaches moral lessons about kindness and generosity, the Ghost plays a crucial role in influencing Scrooge's reflections and potential for personal growth.</data>
      <data key="d5">788fa4df64efbe366465a2d79c57540d,82b4ace78f477e67498bca9fdf85f5c6,a9d95369d1d8bbd8108e7bbd93d652ec,dd956afe0e7b4cef80dab966b9f82753,e63efed56e4606f35b676ab40e2432aa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;JACOB&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Jacob, also known as the Ghost of Christmas Present, plays a guiding role in the narrative by warning and advising Scrooge about his future and moral choices. His presence prompts Scrooge to reflect on mortality and moral responsibility, especially in light of Jacob's death, which leads Scrooge to contemplate the consequences of his actions and the importance of moral integrity.</data>
      <data key="d5">2f4b2f4053b5911c93f7f63677f780fe,a340401c3ec3b6740e9a82b46c6623a4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;MARLEY'S GHOST&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Marley's Ghost is the spirit of Jacob Marley, who appears to Scrooge to warn him about the consequences of greed and to foretell the visits from other spirits. It haunts and warns Scrooge, playing a pivotal role in his supernatural experiences and moral reflection.</data>
      <data key="d5">82b4ace78f477e67498bca9fdf85f5c6,a340401c3ec3b6740e9a82b46c6623a4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE GHOST&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scrooge examines the door and environment after the Ghost's entry, indicating his interaction and investigation of supernatural phenomena. The Ghost guides Scrooge through scenes of his past, present, and future, leading to his moral awakening.</data>
      <data key="d5">696cd4a0ae22e395f3c9d04cfcea8d49,82b4ace78f477e67498bca9fdf85f5c6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;ROOM&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scrooge is present in the room where the ghost appears and interacts with the spirit, seeking to understand its purpose. He observes the transformed room, which has become a scene of Christmas festivity, indicating that he has been exposed to the spirit's message and has experienced a change in his surroundings reflecting the spirit's influence.</data>
      <data key="d5">2398bef3715ca1485012bdd394f7db3e,6359ab00dbbec9c39edad51589aea3ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE SPIRIT&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Spirit guides Scrooge through visions of his past, present, and future, enabling his emotional reflection and change."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;ALI BABA&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge recognizes Ali Baba as a character from a foreign story, expressing recognition and nostalgia."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;VALENTINE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Valentine is part of Scrooge's nostalgic memories, representing childhood stories."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;ORSON&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Orson is remembered as a wild brother in Scrooge's past, part of his storytelling memories."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE SULTAN'S GROOM&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"A character from a story within the narrative, involved in a fantastical scene that Scrooge recalls."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE PARROT&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Parrot is a lively memory from Scrooge's past, symbolizing humor and liveliness."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;ROBIN CRUSOE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Robin Crusoe is a story character that Scrooge recalls, associated with adventure."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE BOY SINGING A CHRISTMAS CAROL&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The boy represents innocence and the festive spirit, recalling a Christmas memory."</data>
      <data key="d5">e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;FAN&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fan is a young girl who visits her uncle, Scrooge, to bring him home for Christmas, symbolizing familial love and warmth. Her visit signifies a moment of familial reunion and hope for a better future, which impacts Scrooge's emotional state. Fan is described as Scrooge's younger sister or relative, and her presence emphasizes themes of family, love, and hope during the holiday season.</data>
      <data key="d5">33439623114354941f9323e7d62c790b,e6179d70c9e4e7bbf0b591e9ece706e8</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;FEZZIWIG&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fezziwig is a former employer of Scrooge, known for his festive and generous nature. He is the host of the Christmas celebration that Scrooge attends, which signifies a relationship of employer and former apprentice or friend. Fezziwig's joyful and generous celebration has a significant influence on Scrooge's feelings and memories, highlighting a stark contrast between Scrooge's current attitude and his past acts of kindness and generosity.</data>
      <data key="d5">33439623114354941f9323e7d62c790b,3edbf99376540faf00d8f5307941932e,4b5a2c29bc3f579c8040c45f2a9363d0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;DICK WILKINS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Dick Wilkins is a former fellow-apprentice and friend of Scrooge, sharing in the festive activities."</data>
      <data key="d5">3edbf99376540faf00d8f5307941932e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;SPIRIT&quot;">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
      <data key="d4">In the narrative involving Scrooge and the Spirit, the Spirit plays a pivotal role in guiding and influencing Scrooge through a series of visions of his past, present, and future. These visions serve to deepen Scrooge's understanding of his life choices, morality, and mortality, ultimately inspiring a moral transformation. Throughout their interactions, Scrooge engages with the Spirit by questioning its motives and expressing concern about deprivation, reflecting a moral and philosophical relationship. He seeks insight into Tiny Tim's fate and reflects on his own morality, demonstrating a desire for self-awareness and change. The Spirit's guidance is instrumental in shaping Scrooge's emotional response and moral awakening, emphasizing lessons about the importance of compassion, the consequences of greed, and the value of human kindness. Overall, the relationship between Scrooge and the Spirit is characterized by guidance, influence, and a shared goal of moral redemption.</data>
      <data key="d5">25293d3af56b1e8bb7387f18e5f830c6,2c53885ea36c9f539ca623101d70b60b,2f4b2f4053b5911c93f7f63677f780fe,312a70793b39dccac6fb4e4b0d23df0d,6b5812a07d880d92f055d038934b2b87,788fa4df64efbe366465a2d79c57540d,f12da31d40e34efbe8c2c848cff576db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;SPIRIT OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT&quot;">
      <data key="d3">3.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The Spirit of Christmas Present appears to Ebenezer Scrooge, guiding him through scenes of Christmas celebration and imparting moral lessons. It shows Scrooge the current state of others during Christmas, highlighting both the joy and suffering experienced by people around him. The Spirit of Christmas Present interacts with Scrooge to teach him the importance of kindness and generosity, encouraging him to embrace the true spirit of Christmas.</data>
      <data key="d5">2398bef3715ca1485012bdd394f7db3e,3e0edc9c6b1dbff5eaeaf248e6181926,7c3c14fb7534245699334c3f3af741d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;CITY STREETS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge observes the city streets during Christmas morning, witnessing the festive atmosphere and community spirit."|</data>
      <data key="d5">3e0edc9c6b1dbff5eaeaf248e6181926</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;FRED&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Fred is Scrooge's nephew who actively celebrates Christmas and endeavors to influence Scrooge's attitude towards the holiday. He strives to bring joy and a festive spirit to Scrooge, embodying familial love and hope.</data>
      <data key="d5">25293d3af56b1e8bb7387f18e5f830c6,788fa4df64efbe366465a2d79c57540d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;CHRISTMAS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Christmas serves as the moral and spiritual turning point for Scrooge, emphasizing themes of generosity and redemption."</data>
      <data key="d5">e63efed56e4606f35b676ab40e2432aa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE STREET&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge observes societal interactions and scenes in the street, reflecting societal conditions."</data>
      <data key="d5">2f4b2f4053b5911c93f7f63677f780fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;THE SHOP&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge visits or observes the shop, which is depicted as a place of crime and misery, representing societal decay."</data>
      <data key="d5">2f4b2f4053b5911c93f7f63677f780fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;DEATH&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge reflects on death, experiencing fear and contemplating the moral implications of his life."</data>
      <data key="d5">f12da31d40e34efbe8c2c848cff576db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;TINY TIM&quot;">
      <data key="d3">2.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Scrooge's transformation is significantly motivated by Tiny Tim's plight and well-being, which influence his newfound generosity and concern for others. Tiny Tim's innocence and moral message play a crucial role in inspiring Scrooge to change his ways and become more compassionate.</data>
      <data key="d5">4dd973f86fac64800eb57dc9848d3136,f13768b850451c6d88dc4c794e5c6cf7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;PAST, PRESENT, FUTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge's journey through the past, present, and future spirits leads to his moral awakening and change."</data>
      <data key="d5">4dd973f86fac64800eb57dc9848d3136</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;SPIRIT OF CHRISTMAS PAST&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Spirit of Christmas Past guides Scrooge through memories of his earlier life, illustrating how he became the person he is today."|</data>
      <data key="d5">7c3c14fb7534245699334c3f3af741d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;SPIRIT OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Spirit of Christmas Yet to Come reveals possible future outcomes based on Scrooge's current behavior, urging him to change."|</data>
      <data key="d5">7c3c14fb7534245699334c3f3af741d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;BOY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge interacts with the boy to gather information about local shops and Christmas traditions, showing his engagement with the community."</data>
      <data key="d5">4d24514bfd653ec63181d9d824eb98ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SCROOGE&quot;" target="&quot;TURKEY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Scrooge purchases the turkey as a gesture of generosity for Christmas, symbolizing his transformation."</data>
      <data key="d5">4d24514bfd653ec63181d9d824eb98ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CHRISTMAS EVE&quot;" target="&quot;CLERK&quot;">
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The clerk celebrates Christmas Eve with joy, contrasting with Scrooge's gloom, and this event highlights themes of holiday spirit."|</data>
      <data key="d5">0199efcc183aac09eefc978fa1b715f1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;FEZZIWIG&quot;" target="&quot;WAREHOUSE&quot;">
